    "evidence_text": "Memory and Modularity in Cell-Fate Decision Making\n\nGenetically identical cells sharing an environment can display markedly different phenotypes.\n\nThe transcriptional program of sporulation in budding yeast\n\nAt least seven distinct temporal patterns of induction were observed.\n\nmicroRNAs: A Safeguard against Turmoil?\n\nThe differentiation of the bacterium Bacillus subtilis into a dormant spore is among the most well-characterized developmental pathways in biology.\n\nMore recently, transcriptional profiling uncovered additional sporulation-induced genes required for successful spore development.\n\nSeveral mutants had novel sporulation phenotypes.\n\nMafB/c-Maf deficiency enables self-renewal of differentiated functional macrophages.\n\nIt thus appears possible to amplify functional differentiated cells without malignant transformation or stem cell intermediates.\n\nCell survival during complete nutrient deprivation depends on lipid droplet-fueled \u03b2-oxidation of fatty acids.\n\nGenotoxic Stress Abrogates Renewal of Melanocyte Stem Cells by Triggering Their Differentiation\n\nThe resulting MSC depletion leads to irreversible hair graying.\n\nThis review critically evaluates stem cell research from a molecular chaperone perspective.\n\nPurification and characterization of mouse hematopoietic stem cells.\n\nModeling the evolution of culture-adapted human embryonic stem cells.",
